  <article class="article">
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Check these before buying</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Match the model and region.</strong> Open the exact model’s official downloads, not another printer with a similar name.</li>
        <li><strong>Match the software.</strong> Check your distribution, release, package format, and CPU architecture in the individual download instructions. A “64-bit” label alone does not establish ARM support.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose the connection.</strong> Check the instructions for USB or your local network. Do not assume success on one connection proves the other.</li>
        <li><strong>Check scanning separately.</strong> Find the scanner package and required permissions, then distinguish flatbed, ADF, duplex scanning, scan-button operation, and saving to a network folder. We have not verified all of these for the MFC-J4335DW.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify your routine during the return period.</strong> Print your own document, try duplex if needed, and scan from your actual application and connection. Only describe a workflow as tested after that check.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Does an official driver mean driverless printing?</h2>
      <p>No. A vendor package is one software path. A driverless path needs its own model and protocol evidence and a check on your computer. An AirPrint or network-protocol listing also does not prove that scanning, maintenance, and every print setting work through that same path.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>What about the ET-2850 and G3270?</h2>
      <p>The sources retained in our <a href="/guides/no-subscription-printers/">general buying guide</a> do not establish the complete Linux print-and-scan workflow for either model. That is an evidence gap, not a finding that Linux cannot work. We have not completed a separate Linux package and scanner-support review for these two models.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Need a printer without accounts or subscriptions too?</h2>
      <p>Linux download availability does not answer account, supply, or firmware questions. Compare the <a href="/guides/no-subscription-printers/#accounts-and-offline-use">account and offline-use boundaries</a>, then check the ordinary toner or ink required for your model.</p>
    </section>
